<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="vi">

<head>
    @* Assets are only needed on a full page load. On a fragment request Unpoly is already
       running in the page, so re-sending stylesheets, the bundle and the config script is
       pure waste. <HeadOutlet /> stays outside the wrapper so <PageTitle> keeps working —
       that is what saves us from needing X-Up-Title this early.
       Trade-off: with no assets in <head>, Unpoly cannot detect asset changes on fragment
       responses. Revisit in Phase G. 📖 https://unpoly.com/handling-asset-changes *@
    <UpChrome>
        <meta charset="utf-8" />
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
        <base href="/" />

        @* Same typefaces jubinstudio.com loads. Plain CSS, no Tailwind: a build step and a
           watch process would be a second toolchain in a repo whose workflow is `dotnet run`. *@
        <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
        <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Cormorant+Garamond:ital,wght@0,300;0,400;0,500;1,300&family=DM+Sans:wght@300;400;500&display=swap" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />

        @* Application scripts belong in <head> with [defer]. In <body> they re-execute on
           every swap of that region. 📖 https://unpoly.com/legacy-scripts *@
        <script src="@Assets["js/gallery.js"]" defer></script>
        <script src="@Assets["js/app.js"]" defer></script>

        <UnpolyHead />
    </UpChrome>

    @* OUTSIDE UpChrome on purpose.

       Unpoly only tracks assets in <head>; elements in <body> are never tracked. Cutting the
       whole head on fragment responses therefore switched asset detection off entirely --
       up:assets:changed could never fire for a fragment. Keeping one marker outside the
       wrapper restores it for about a hundred bytes.
       📖 https://unpoly.com/up-asset *@
    <meta name="app-version" content="@AssetVersion" up-asset />

    <HeadOutlet />
</head>

<body>
    <Routes />

    @* blazor.web.js is omitted on purpose.
       With interactivity=None it only provides enhanced navigation and enhanced forms —
       exactly what Unpoly is doing here. Keeping both makes them fight over clicks and submits. *@
</body>

</html>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] public HttpContext? Ctx { get; set; }

    // Read from the query per request. A static would leak the bumped version into every
    // later page, so up:assets:changed fired on navigations that changed nothing.
    string AssetVersion => Ctx?.Request.Query["v"].ToString() == "2" ? "2" : "1";
}
